twin bench, stage 0: R1's rail line in openEMS against its 2-D section (docs/KNOTTED_GUIDE.md 8.1, DR-0)
registered before any run (DR-0): the resonance within 0.5% and Q within 15% of full wave
run: full; engine gpu; seconds section 65, straight 1101, ring 675

== the section as modelled ==
  one 1.6 mm FR-4 board (er 4.4, tan d 0.02) with 35 um copper: a centre strip and rails of 0.25 and 1.31 mm of copper between 0.15 mm gaps centred on ELE-1's edges c1 = 0.20 and c2 = 1.66 mm (a = 0.40, b = 1.46 mm); a ground plane under the board, via walls on the centre plane and 1.0 mm beyond the outer gap, a grounded shield 25 mm out
  the null 0.571 mm above the copper's top face (ELE-1's gapless sqrt(c1 c2) = 0.576 mm over the plane), its gradient 1.039 of ELE-1's: q 0.5 takes 48.1 V where ELE-1 says 50 V
  left floating, the strip takes 0.850 of the rails' volts on this board and 0.923 on the board as written (no plane); the null then sits 0.087 mm above the copper on this board and nowhere on that one: the via wall that holds the strip at 0 V is part of the design. Floating, the line has two modes, n_eff 1.5634 and 1.6695

== calibration (rule 5) ==
  Wheeler's rule on stripline 1.0, 0.035, 1.6 mm (width, thickness, spacing): Z0 84.2 ohm, alpha_c 1.010 of Pozar's closed form
  Wheeler's rule on stripline 2.0, 0.018, 1.0 mm (width, thickness, spacing): Z0 37.5 ohm, alpha_c 0.993 of Pozar's closed form
  Wheeler's rule on stripline 1.0, 0.018, 1.0 mm (width, thickness, spacing): Z0 63.1 ohm, alpha_c 0.999 of Pozar's closed form
  Pozar's eq. 3.198 is itself Wheeler's rule applied to a closed-form Z0(t): the stripline agreement shows the receded solve's numerical derivative right, so the copper's loss is held to Wheeler's closed form only
  the ring-down fit (matrix pencil) on a damped tone, Q 60, with a second tone and 1e-3 noise: f to +2.5e-06, Q to -6.2e-04
  the face integral of the static field, R_s |E_n|^2 on the edge beside each copper face (the 3-D integral's quasi-static twin), against the cell at the copper: Q_c 119.9 at 4.37 um, Q_c 112.8 at 2.19 um, Q_c 107.8 at 1.09 um, Q_c 103.9 at 0.55 um; Wheeler's rule 93.5. The face integral converges slowly, the corners' fields going as r^(-1/3), so a copper loss read off a grid's faces is calibrated by its static twin on those cells
  extrapolated to no cell: its differences fall 0.78 per halving at the finest (2^(-1/3) is 0.79, the corners' r^(-2/3)); Richardson at 2^(-1/3) puts its limit at 88.8 and Aitken's on the last three at 90.3, 5.0% and 3.5% under Wheeler's rule's 93.5: the one check of the copper's loss here that does not use the rule; a surface-impedance solve (Palace) is DR-0's item for it
  Wheeler's rule moves 0.03% with far cells of 0.5 mm instead of 1 mm or the shield at 50 mm

