== R1's ring, full wave (fine mesh, cylindrical: every edge on a mesh line, no staircase) ==
  centre-line radius 31.416 mm, 197.39 mm round (m = 1 at 915 MHz by the even mode's n_eff); a quarter between a magnetic wall at alpha 0 and an electric wall at pi/2, 35 alpha lines: 0.75 M cells, 3.22 M steps, 11.3 min
  dt 9.30837e-15 s: the laminate's damping per step 17.957 units of 2^-24, held as 18: the run's laminate loses 1.0024 of the given loss
  the single-mode decomposed model puts m = 1 at 915.000 MHz with the rails in phase; full wave finds no such mode:
    mode A: 899.310 MHz (-1.71% from 915), the inner rail at +0.287 of the outer (-0.4 deg); Q_d 60.09 (61.29 at tan d 0.02, the rounding out), Q_c 124.2 (H dump), Q 41.04; each rail's two gaps 1.0004 apart; fit residual 2.0e-05
    mode B: 962.161 MHz (+5.15% from 915), the inner rail at -3.943 of the outer (-179.5 deg); Q_d 65.61 (62.55 at tan d 0.02, the rounding out), Q_c 130.5 (H dump), Q 42.28; each rail's two gaps 0.9971 apart; fit residual 3.0e-05
  why: the rails' centre lines are 191.5 and 203.2 mm round (-+2.96%), while the strip's via wall leaves them coupled by C12/C11 -0.020 and their even and odd indices 1.6599 and 1.6041 differ by 3.36%: curvature mixes the even mode with the odd as strongly as the section separates them, so each ring mode lives mostly on one rail
  the two-rail decomposed model (the rails' 2 x 2 line matrices from the converged section, each rail per radian on its own radius):
    mode A: 899.866 MHz, the inner rail at +0.285 of the outer, Q_d 61.36, Q_c 96.0 (Wheeler), Q 37.44; full wave against it: f -0.062%, the rails' ratio +0.002, Q_d -0.13%; on its own cells' twin: f +0.043%, Q_d +0.03%, Q_c +0.07%
    mode B: 963.592 MHz, the inner rail at -3.997 of the outer, Q_d 62.63, Q_c 101.4 (Wheeler), Q 38.72; full wave against it: f -0.149%, the rails' ratio +0.054, Q_d -0.13%; on its own cells' twin: f -0.037%, Q_d +0.04%, Q_c -0.17%
  DR-0, ring mode A, single-mode model: resonance -1.71%: FAIL (band 0.5%); the rails not in phase
  DR-0, ring mode A, two-rail model: resonance -0.06%: pass (band 0.5%); Q, the copper off these cells: +9.62%: pass (band 15.0%); calibrated (x0.773): -0.05%: pass (band 15.0%)
  DR-0, ring mode B, single-mode model: resonance +5.15%: FAIL (band 0.5%); the rails not in phase
  DR-0, ring mode B, two-rail model: resonance -0.15%: pass (band 0.5%); Q, the copper off these cells: +9.19%: pass (band 15.0%); calibrated (x0.776): -0.15%: pass (band 15.0%)
  Hypothesis (read from these results, not yet tested): the null needs the rails in phase, so R1's ring needs them held together against the curvature: bridges joining the rails across the strip at N >= 3 stations a lap (N not dividing 2m), the rails then slipping apart in phase by at most 2 pi m (c1 + c2) / (2 R N) midway between bridges, 0.031 rad at N = 6; or two compiled feeds that drive both ring modes into the in-phase pattern at 915 MHz (section 5.3), at the power the off-resonant mode costs

== what limits the agreement ==
  staircasing: none; the straight line's copper and the ring's (in r and z, the cylindrical mesh) lie on mesh lines, and the magnetic walls sit on dual lines placed at the centre plane and the line's ends (openEMS puts a magnetic wall half a cell inside the boundary)
  mesh: the FDTD's cells at the copper set n_eff, Z0 and the face integral; its own 2-D twin on the same cells carries the same error (the full wave adds the rest)
  ports: none; a soft source and probes, the line closed by mirror walls, so no end correction enters: the two walls' volts agree to 1e-6 and the middle reads what cos puts there
  precision: openEMS keeps its update coefficients in single precision, so a lossy cell's damping per step is a whole number of 2^-24 and the laminate's loss is off by up to 2^-25 / (dt sigma / eps) of itself: 2.7 % for FR-4 at the fine mesh's dt, growing as tan d times dt falls (low-loss laminates, finer cells); computed from dt and taken out above, it is a limit to register for every lossy openEMS run
  the copper: openEMS holds PEC copper of its real thickness; its loss is the surface integral of the full-wave H, which on 5 to 10 um cells reads the copper's Q high (its static twin shows by how much), and the via walls and the shield carry none in either model; the shield's PEC carries no radiation either. The copper's treated face, its roughness of the order of the 2.2 um skin depth, takes Hammerstad's factor on its share of the loss (array16.guide.sections, Section.rough), and the unbacked board as R1 draws it is the next full-wave ring
